meta {
  name: 06 Payer goes straight to AHDS - EXPECT 403
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

get {
  url: {{fhirA}}/Patient?_count=5
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  _count: 5
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{tokA}}
  Accept: application/fhir+json
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 403
}

docs {
  Assertion 12. The one that closes the "can they just go around it" question.

  No APIM in this request at all - the URL is the FHIR service hostname
  directly, and the token is payer A's own valid token.

  403, not 401. Read the difference out loud: Entra authenticated the caller
  perfectly well. AHDS then looked for a role assignment for that principal
  and found none, because the payer applications hold zero Azure role
  assignments. The only principal with FHIR Data Contributor is the APIM
  managed identity - shown in portal tab 5.

  Skipping the gateway does not skip the control. It removes the only means
  of access.
}
